{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Full Dev Pipeline",
  "description": "A manually triggered master pipeline that runs the full development workflow: 1) Fetch a GitHub issue and create a Kiro spec with requirements, design, and tasks, 2) Implement the code based on the spec tasks, 3) Review code against project standards, 4) Run the app locally to demonstrate the changes.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "userTriggered"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "Execute the full development pipeline in order. Complete each step fully before moving to the next. Report progress after each step.\n\n**Step 1 — Get GitHub Issue & Create Task & Spec**\n\n1. Use the GitHub MCP tool (mcp_github_list_issues) to pull all open issues from the repository (owner: Piashsarker, repo: inec-election-results).\n2. List the open issues and ask the user which issue to work on (or pick the most relevant one if context is clear).\n3. Read the selected issue details using mcp_github_get_issue.\n4. Create a new spec directory: `.kiro/specs/issue-<number>-<short-slug>/`\n5. Generate three spec files based on the issue:\n   - `requirements.md` — Extract functional and non-functional requirements from the issue. Use numbered requirements (REQ-1, REQ-2, etc.) with clear acceptance criteria.\n   - `design.md` — Define the technical design: components to create/modify, data flow, API changes, database changes, and key design decisions. Reference the existing project architecture from .kiro/steering/structure.md.\n   - `tasks.md` — Break the implementation into ordered, atomic tasks. Each task should be a single unit of work (create file, modify endpoint, add component, etc.) with clear done criteria.\n6. Report the spec summary to the user.\n\n**Step 2 — Write Code**\n\n1. Read the tasks.md file from the spec created in Step 1.\n2. Work through each task in order:\n   - Read any relevant existing code before making changes.\n   - Implement the task following project conventions (async-first backend, App Router frontend, proper type annotations).\n   - Follow the design decisions from design.md.\n   - Mark each task as complete as you go.\n3. After all tasks are done, run linting to catch issues:\n   - Python: `cd backend && python -m ruff check . 2>&1` (fix with `ruff check --fix .` if needed)\n   - TypeScript: `cd frontend && npx next lint 2>&1`\n4. Run tests if applicable: `cd backend && python -m pytest tests/ --tb=short -q 2>&1`\n5. Report what was implemented.\n\n**Step 3 — Code Review with Standards**\n\n1. Run `git diff` to see all changes made.\n2. Review every modified/created file against these standards:\n   - **Architecture**: Follows project patterns (FastAPI + async SQLAlchemy backend, Next.js App Router frontend, Radix UI components)\n   - **Type Safety**: Full type annotations (Python type hints, TypeScript strict mode)\n   - **Error Handling**: Uses custom exceptions from backend/app/core/errors.py, proper try/catch in frontend\n   - **Security**: No exposed secrets, parameterized queries, validated inputs, no XSS vectors\n   - **Code Style**: Matches ruff/black config (line-length=100, py311 target), ESLint rules for frontend\n   - **Spec Alignment**: Implementation matches the design.md decisions and satisfies requirements.md criteria\n   - **Accessibility**: Frontend components have proper ARIA attributes, keyboard navigation\n3. Fix any issues found during review.\n4. Report the review summary — what passed, what was fixed.\n\n**Step 4 — Run App Locally to Show Changes**\n\n1. Start infrastructure services: `docker compose up -d` in workspace root. Wait for containers to be healthy.\n2. Run database migrations if any were added: `cd backend && alembic upgrade head`\n3. Start the backend FastAPI server using controlBashProcess (action: start): `source .venv/bin/activate && uvicorn app.main:app --reload --port 8000` in the backend/ directory.\n4. Start the frontend Next.js dev server using controlBashProcess (action: start): `npm run dev` in the frontend/ directory.\n5. Verify both servers are running by checking process output.\n6. Report the running URLs and describe how to see the changes:\n   - Backend API: http://localhost:8000\n   - API Docs: http://localhost:8000/docs\n   - Frontend: http://localhost:3000\n   - Specific page/endpoint to visit to see the new feature\n7. If any step fails, report the error and suggest fixes.\n\nAfter all 4 steps complete, provide a final summary: issue addressed, spec created, code implemented, review passed, and where to see the running feature."
  }
}
